From 3badede6e1f96acb9247d4c5d818e9ff9bccd1da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Karel Zak <kzak@redhat.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 18:55:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 29/32] libmount: restrict the mount source for non-root users
 [CVE-2026-78410]

Two ways in which the source of a restricted (non-root) mount was
resolved with root privileges, both of them following symlinks the
unprivileged user controls:

 * mnt_context_prepare_srcpath() canonicalized every source with
   mnt_resolve_path(), which is realpath() running as euid 0.  A disk
   image in a user writable directory could therefore be swapped for a
   symlink to anything.  Only /dev/ paths are canonicalized now -- that
   is what /dev/cdrom -> /dev/sr0 needs -- and the result has to stay
   in /dev/, otherwise the original fstab path is kept.  For all other
   sources the fstab path is used as it is; the symlink protection is
   done at open time by RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS.

 * mnt_context_open_tree() called open_tree(AT_FDCWD, path, ...), which
   resolves the path in the kernel and follows symlinks in every
   component.  For a bind or move operation the fstab authorized source
   could thus be redirected to an arbitrary directory.  Combined with
   X-mount.owner=, X-mount.group= or X-mount.mode= this is an ownership
   and permission modification primitive on paths the user was never
   authorized for, because the post-mount hook applies chown/chmod with
   root privileges.

The new mnt_open_tree() helper pins the path with
openat2(RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS) first and then clones it with
open_tree(fd, "", AT_EMPTY_PATH).  Restricted callers ask for the
resolve flags, root passes resolve=0 and keeps the plain open_tree()
behaviour.  The fallback open_tree() in hook_idmap.c is converted too.

This completes the pinning started for the target in the previous
commit; the legacy mount(2) path already pins the bind/move source
there.

Backport notes:

 * The patches are taken from the upstream stable/v2.42 branch; each of
   the commits referenced below records the corresponding master commit
   in its own message.

 * ul_startswith() is still called startswith() here.

 * utils.c newly needs open_tree(), so it includes mount-api-utils.h.
   Upstream relies on the declaration coming from glibc's <sys/mount.h>;
   the explicit include keeps the build working with older glibc, the
   same way hook_mount.c does it.

 * a323dddbc is a no-op here, hook_idmap.c already includes
   fileutils.h.

 * The upstream mount.8.adoc hunk does not apply -- this branch has no
   X-mount.nocanonicalize documentation.  The behaviour change is
   described in the "user" section instead, next to the target pinning
   note added by the previous commit.

CVE-2026-78410

